<zk>
	<label multiline="true">
		For each timebox:
		1. Click the timebox, select all of its text and type 0, then click outside the timebox. The text should be 00:00, AM 12:00, 12:00:00 AM and 0000. The last timebox shows an error and keeps 0 :.
		2. Click the up button twice (once for the last timebox). The field at the cursor should step: 00:01 then 00:02, AM 12:01 then AM 12:02, 12:00:00 PM then 12:00:00 AM, 0001 then 0002, and 01: for the last timebox.
		Bug: the digits go in at the wrong position (0001:00, 00100, 01 :), or the AM/PM part is lost (12:00:01).
		3. For the first timebox, click outside again. The date in the zk log should be the same as after step 1.
		4. Click the first timebox, put the cursor after the hour, press Backspace, then click outside. The text should be 00:02.
		Bug: the minutes are lost (00:00).
		5. For the first timebox, select all and type 1, click outside, then click the up button. The text should be 01:00, then 01:01.
		Bug: 0101:00.
		6. For the first timebox, select all and type 0, click outside, then click the down button twice. The text should be 00:00, 00:59, then 00:58.
		Bug: 0059:00, then 005959:00.
	</label>
	<timebox format="HH:mm" onChange='Clients.log(new java.text.SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss").format(self.value))'/>
	<timebox format="a hh:mm" locale="en_US"/>
	<timebox format="h:mm:ss a" locale="en_US"/>
	<timebox format="HHmm"/>
	<timebox format="HH:mm" constraint="after 090000"/>
</zk>
